### Description

`SegmentCommitInfo.sizeInBytes()` caches its result in a volatile field whose `-1` value means “not computed yet”:

https://github.com/apache/lucene/blob/12d316cb85463e4bd0bce550077216a7c5dbb9b1/lucene/core/src/java/org/apache/lucene/index/SegmentCommitInfo.java#L269-L280

`generationAdvanced()` invalidates that cache by assigning -1.

https://github.com/apache/lucene/blob/12d316cb85463e4bd0bce550077216a7c5dbb9b1/lucene/core/src/java/org/apache/lucene/index/SegmentCommitInfo.java#L457-L460

If a thread computes and stores a size, and another thread advances the generation before the first thread executes the final volatile read, then `sizeInBytes()` returns -1 instead of a size.

There is also a separate best-effort-cache race: if invalidation occurs while a size is being computed, the completed computation can overwrite the invalidation with a size derived from the earlier segment state.

The proposed small fix is to load the cached value into a local variable, compute into that local when needed, publish it once, and return the local. This guarantees that a successful call never returns the -1 sentinel:

```java
long size = sizeInBytes;
if (size == -1) {
  size = 0;
  for (String fileName : files()) {
    size += info.dir.fileLength(fileName);
  }
  sizeInBytes = size;
}
return size;
```

This does not make `SegmentCommitInfo` thread-safe or guarantee that a size computed concurrently with mutations is current. In particular, `files()` still reads mutable collections and callers requiring a consistent segment state must retain the appropriate writer-side synchronization. It only restores the intended property that callers never receive the cache sentinel as a size.


### Version and environment details

10.5.2

