### Search before asking

- [x] I searched in the [issues](https://github.com/apache/paimon/issues) and found nothing similar.


### Motivation

[PIP-41](https://cwiki.apache.org/confluence/display/PAIMON/PIP-41%3A+Introduce+FilePath+Global+Index+And+Optimizations+For+Lookup+In+Append+Table) proposes file-path-based global indexes to speed up lookups on append tables. File index can offer a complementary capability at file granularity: while a data file is written, a `FileIndexWriter` receives the indexed value of every row in row order, so an index implementation could produce key -> (data file, row position) entries during index construction and feed them into a cross-file lookup structure. This issue only asks for the missing building block, not for an implementation of PIP-41.

This is not possible today: the writer is created by `FileIndexer#createWriter()` without knowing which data file it indexes, although `DataFileIndexWriter` is always constructed right after the data file path is decided.

Current workarounds are fragile:
- passing the path through a `ThreadLocal`, which depends on internal construction order;
- wrapping the file format to get `FileAwareFormatWriter#setFile`, which changes the data file extension and requires registering the custom format on every reader;
- resolving files in a `CommitCallback`, which only sees the files after commit and needs an extra token-to-file indirection.

Paimon already exposes the file location at write time elsewhere: `FileAwareFormatWriter#setFile` for format writers, and `TableWrite#withBlobConsumer` (#7074), which reports the uri, offset and length of each blob as it is written.

### Solution

Add a small context class and a default method. Existing `FileIndexer` implementations keep working through the default fallback:

```java
public class FileIndexWriterContext {
    public Path dataFilePath();  // the data file indexed by the writer
    public long schemaId();      // the table schema the data file is written with
}

public interface FileIndexer {
    FileIndexWriter createWriter();

    default FileIndexWriter createWriter(FileIndexWriterContext context) {
        return createWriter();
    }
}
```

The schema id lets plugins associate these file references with the table schema of the data file, including when the indexes of older files are rebuilt.

`DataFileIndexWriter` passes the context to all index writers, including nested map index writers. Every call site provides it: `RowDataFileWriter`, `KeyValueDataFileWriter`, `KeyValueClusteringFileWriter` and `FileIndexProcessor`, so `rewrite_file_index` also provides it for existing files.

- Built-in indexes and the index file format are unchanged.
- Existing `FileIndexer` implementations are unaffected; only the internal `DataFileIndexWriter` constructor and factory methods now take the context.

### Anything else?

_No response_

### Are you willing to submit a PR?

- [x] I'm willing to submit a PR!

