body_text:
### Description

`StatisticsBrief.getTPValue` scans the histogram buckets from the highest down to find the percentile bucket, but the loop condition excludes bucket 0:

```java
// common/src/main/java/org/apache/rocketmq/common/statistics/StatisticsBrief.java:115
int tmp = 0;
for (int i = counts.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    tmp += counts[i].get();
    if (tmp > excludes) {
        return Math.min(getSlotTPValue(i), getMax());
    }
}
return 0;
```

When all (or enough) samples fall into the first bucket, the scan never accumulates more than `excludes` and the method falls through to `return 0` — a percentile of 0 for a non-empty, non-zero sample set.

Reproduction with the meta `{{100, 10}}` (range [0,100), 10 slots): sample the value `5` one hundred times. `getMax()` is 5, but `getTPValue(0.99f)` and `tp999()` return **0**, because every sample lands in bucket 0 (`(5 - 0) / 10 == 0`), which the loop never visits.

`StatisticsBrief` backs `StatisticsBriefInterceptor` / `StatisticsManager` percentile output (e.g. TP999 printed by `StatisticsItemScheduledIncrementPrinter`), so any metric whose whole population sits in the first latency bucket reports a zero percentile.

### Expected behavior

The traversal should include bucket 0, i.e. loop while `i >= 0`; a sample set contained in the first bucket then yields `min(slot-0 upper bound, max)` instead of 0.

