body_text:
### Description

The request-reply example `example/src/main/java/org/apache/rocketmq/example/rpc/ResponseConsumer.java` acknowledges every request as successfully consumed even when sending the reply failed:

```java
try {
    ...
    SendResult replyResult = replyProducer.send(replyMessage, 3000);
    System.out.printf("reply to %s , %s %n", replyTo, replyResult.toString());
} catch (MQClientException | RemotingException | MQBrokerException | InterruptedException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
...
return ConsumeConcurrentlyStatus.CONSUME_SUCCESS;
```

When the reply send throws (broker briefly unavailable, network hiccup), the exception is only printed and the listener still returns `CONSUME_SUCCESS`. The request message is removed from the queue, the requester never receives a reply, and its `request()` call eventually times out — even though a redelivery would very likely have produced the reply. `InterruptedException` additionally swallows the interrupt status.

The same flawed snippet is embedded in `docs/cn/rpc_request.md` (lines ~84-88).

### Expected behavior

On a failed reply send the example should return `RECONSUME_LATER` so the request is redelivered and the reply retried (matching the error handling of the simple consumer examples); on `InterruptedException` it should restore the interrupt flag before returning. The doc copy should be updated to match.

