body_text:
### Description

`SyncStateInfo` (`controller/src/main/java/org/apache/rocketmq/controller/impl/manager/SyncStateInfo.java`) maintains the controller's per-broker view of mastership: master identity + epoch, and the sync-state set + epoch. It deliberately copies sets on the way in and out:

```java
public void updateSyncStateSetInfo(Set<Long> newSyncStateSet) {
    this.syncStateSet = new HashSet<>(newSyncStateSet);
    ...
}
public Set<Long> getSyncStateSet() {
    return new HashSet<>(syncStateSet);
}
```

The existing manager-level tests touch the class via larger flows, but no test pins the defensive-copy contract itself: mutating the caller's set after `updateSyncStateSetInfo` (or mutating the set returned by the getter) must not corrupt controller state, `updateMasterInfo` must bump the master epoch, and `removeFromSyncState` must remove locally without advancing the sync-state epoch. Losing any of these (e.g. "simplifying" away the copies) would silently let callers mutate controller state and currently no test would notice.

### Expected behavior

Direct unit coverage of the isolation and epoch semantics described above.

