### Search before asking

- [x] Searched existing issues and PRs for `LOGGED_EDGE_OVERRIDE_ISSUES`, `ObservabilityConfig`, and observability warning-cache leaks. No matching report or fix was found.

### What happened

The Zeta observability configuration parser retains every distinct invalid `edge_overrides` warning key in a process-wide static set. Those keys survive after the corresponding job/configuration is gone. The set has no size bound, expiry, or lifecycle cleanup.

This is reproduced with the unchanged `ObservabilityConfig.java` from `dev` commit `75fd4ed4b2e63579a57b461285997951490c4fe7`, not inferred from an older release. The reproduction ran on a Linux test host (bigdata3), OpenJDK 8u502, in a separate JVM with `-Xmx256m` and a 60-second timeout. It invokes the production public parser, without starting or changing an existing cluster.

### Trigger and impact

Repeated configuration parsing with distinct invalid boundary/capacity combinations grows retained heap. For example, each job may contain a different boundary name and a negative capacity. The parser ignores the invalid override but permanently retains the full warning key. This also happens with `engine.observability.enabled=false`, because edge overrides are parsed before the configuration is returned.

The growth depends on the number and length of distinct warning keys. Repeated identical inputs do not grow the set, and ordinary valid configurations do not trigger this finding. The observed defect is unbounded heap retention; the bounded experiment did not attempt to cause an OOM or a cluster outage.

### Source evidence

- [Static set](https://github.com/apache/seatunnel/blob/75fd4ed4b2e63579a57b461285997951490c4fe7/seatunnel-engine/seatunnel-engine-server/src/main/java/org/apache/seatunnel/engine/server/observability/ObservabilityConfig.java#L49): `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()`.
- [Parsing](https://github.com/apache/seatunnel/blob/75fd4ed4b2e63579a57b461285997951490c4fe7/seatunnel-engine/seatunnel-engine-server/src/main/java/org/apache/seatunnel/engine/server/observability/ObservabilityConfig.java#L253): warning keys include boundary names, capacity values, or supplied map keys.
- [Retention](https://github.com/apache/seatunnel/blob/75fd4ed4b2e63579a57b461285997951490c4fe7/seatunnel-engine/seatunnel-engine-server/src/main/java/org/apache/seatunnel/engine/server/observability/ObservabilityConfig.java#L315): `warnOnce()` adds the full key to the static set with no removal or bound.
- Production callers include the `ExecutionPlanGenerator` constructor (line 72), `PhysicalPlanGenerator` (line 159), and `RealtimeMetricsService` (line 318), all passing job env options to `fromEnvOptions()`.

Retention path: loaded `ObservabilityConfig` class -> static `LOGGED_EDGE_OVERRIDE_ISSUES` -> `ConcurrentHashMap.KeySetView` -> map node -> warning-key `String` and backing characters. No job object is required to keep these strings alive.

### Reproduction

Run the following with the engine-server test/runtime classpath on the specified commit. Reflection only observes the set; it does not insert or remove entries. Each parser invocation builds a fresh env map. Returning a weak reference allows the caller to verify that the last returned configuration is collectable.

```java
import org.apache.seatunnel.engine.server.observability.ObservabilityConfig;
import java.lang.ref.WeakReference;
import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.util.*;

public class ObservabilityCacheRepro {
    static WeakReference<ObservabilityConfig> parse(int start, int count, int capacity) {
        ObservabilityConfig last = null;
        String padding = String.join("", Collections.nCopies(128, "x"));
        for (int i = start; i < start + count; i++) {
            Map<String, Object> item = new HashMap<>();
            item.put("boundary", "source-" + i + "-" + padding);
            item.put("capacity", capacity);
            Map<String, Object> env = new HashMap<>();
            env.put("engine.observability.enabled", false);
            env.put("engine.observability.edge_overrides", Collections.singletonList(item));
            last = ObservabilityConfig.fromEnvOptions(env, "job-" + i, 0L);
        }
        return new WeakReference<>(last);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Field f = ObservabilityConfig.class.getDeclaredField("LOGGED_EDGE_OVERRIDE_ISSUES");
        f.setAccessible(true);
        Set<?> keys = (Set<?>) f.get(null);
        System.out.println("baseline=" + keys.size());
        parse(0, 5000, 32);
        System.out.println("valid control=" + keys.size());
        for (int batch = 0; batch < 3; batch++) {
            WeakReference<?> ref = parse(batch * 5000, 5000, -1);
            for (int retry = 0; retry < 10 && ref.get() != null; retry++) {
                System.gc();
                Thread.sleep(100);
            }
            if (ref.get() != null) throw new AssertionError("GC evidence inconclusive");
            System.out.println("batch=" + batch + ", collected=true, retained=" + keys.size());
        }
        parse(0, 15000, -1);
        System.out.println("repeat control=" + keys.size());
    }
}
```

Observed results from the equivalent harness, which additionally counts retained characters and asserts each expected size:

```text
baseline: retainedKeys=0, retainedKeyChars=0
5000 distinct valid configurations: retainedKeys=0, retainedKeyChars=0
invalid batch 1, config collected=true: retainedKeys=5000, retainedKeyChars=833890
invalid batch 2, config collected=true: retainedKeys=10000, retainedKeyChars=1668890
invalid batch 3, config collected=true: retainedKeys=15000, retainedKeyChars=2508890
repeat same 15000 configurations: retainedKeys=15000, retainedKeyChars=2508890
```

The exact production source compiled for this experiment has SHA-256 `89bc9cbcebf69133d0f6dada65e34be04720090d406987c04b45264b00b34edc`. Existing SeaTunnel runtime dependencies were reused; the class under investigation was recompiled from the above dev source, not reused from an older binary.

### Expected behavior / fix direction

Warning suppression should have bounded memory usage independent of historical job count and input-string cardinality. Use a bounded/expiring cache, or a finite warning-category key set with appropriate rate limiting. Preserve current validation, capacity clamping, enablement, and diagnostic behavior. Regression coverage should include distinct invalid inputs, valid inputs, repeats, and concurrent parsing.

### Version / engine

- Version: `dev` at `75fd4ed4b2e63579a57b461285997951490c4fe7`
- Engine: Zeta
- Java: OpenJDK 8u502 on Linux
- Scope: production parser reproduction with GC controls; no end-to-end cluster OOM claim

