### Reproduction

Run the following with the engine-server test/runtime classpath on the specified commit. Reflection only observes the set; it does not insert or remove entries. Each parser invocation builds a fresh env map. Returning a weak reference allows the caller to verify that the last returned configuration is collectable.

```java
import org.apache.seatunnel.engine.server.observability.ObservabilityConfig;
import java.lang.ref.WeakReference;
import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import java.util.*;

public class ObservabilityCacheRepro {
    static WeakReference<ObservabilityConfig> parse(int start, int count, int capacity) {
        ObservabilityConfig last = null;
        String padding = String.join("", Collections.nCopies(128, "x"));
        for (int i = start; i < start + count; i++) {
            Map<String, Object> item = new HashMap<>();
            item.put("boundary", "source-" + i + "-" + padding);
            item.put("capacity", capacity);
            Map<String, Object> env = new HashMap<>();
            env.put("engine.observability.enabled", false);
            env.put("engine.observability.edge_overrides", Collections.singletonList(item));
            last = ObservabilityConfig.fromEnvOptions(env, "job-" + i, 0L);
        }
        return new WeakReference<>(last);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Field f = ObservabilityConfig.class.getDeclaredField("LOGGED_EDGE_OVERRIDE_ISSUES");
        f.setAccessible(true);
        Set<?> keys = (Set<?>) f.get(null);
        System.out.println("baseline=" + keys.size());
        parse(0, 5000, 32);
        System.out.println("valid control=" + keys.size());
        for (int batch = 0; batch < 3; batch++) {
            WeakReference<?> ref = parse(batch * 5000, 5000, -1);
            for (int retry = 0; retry < 10 && ref.get() != null; retry++) {
                System.gc();
                Thread.sleep(100);
            }
            if (ref.get() != null) throw new AssertionError("GC evidence inconclusive");
            System.out.println("batch=" + batch + ", collected=true, retained=" + keys.size());
        }
        parse(0, 15000, -1);
        System.out.println("repeat control=" + keys.size());
    }
}
```

Observed results from the equivalent harness, which additionally counts retained characters and asserts each expected size:

```text
baseline: retainedKeys=0, retainedKeyChars=0
5000 distinct valid configurations: retainedKeys=0, retainedKeyChars=0
invalid batch 1, config collected=true: retainedKeys=5000, retainedKeyChars=833890
invalid batch 2, config collected=true: retainedKeys=10000, retainedKeyChars=1668890
invalid batch 3, config collected=true: retainedKeys=15000, retainedKeyChars=2508890
repeat same 15000 configurations: retainedKeys=15000, retainedKeyChars=2508890
```

The exact production source compiled for this experiment has SHA-256 `89bc9cbcebf69133d0f6dada65e34be04720090d406987c04b45264b00b34edc`. Existing SeaTunnel runtime dependencies were reused; the class under investigation was recompiled from the above dev source, not reused from an older binary.

### Expected behavior / fix direction

Warning suppression should have bounded memory usage independent of historical job count and input-string cardinality. Use a bounded/expiring cache, or a finite warning-category key set with appropriate rate limiting. Preserve current validation, capacity clamping, enablement, and diagnostic behavior. Regression coverage should include distinct invalid inputs, valid inputs, repeats, and concurrent parsing.

