## Search before asking

- [x] Searched existing issues and pull requests; no existing fix for this reproduction was found.

## What happened

In Zeta checkpoint coordinator (`seatunnel-engine/seatunnel-engine-server/src/main/java/org/apache/seatunnel/engine/server/checkpoint/CheckpointCoordinator.java`), `completePendingCheckpoint()` cleans up old checkpoints as follows:

```java
if (completedCheckpointIds.size()
        % coordinatorConfig.getStorage().getMaxRetainedCheckpoints()
        == 0
    && completedCheckpointIds.size()
            / coordinatorConfig.getStorage().getMaxRetainedCheckpoints()
        > 1) {
    List<String> needDeleteCheckpointId = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0;
            i < coordinatorConfig.getStorage().getMaxRetainedCheckpoints();
            i++) {
        needDeleteCheckpointId.add(completedCheckpointIds.removeFirst());
    }
    checkpointStorage.deleteCheckpoint(
            String.valueOf(completedCheckpoint.getJobId()),
            String.valueOf(completedCheckpoint.getPipelineId()),
            needDeleteCheckpointId);
}
```

`CHECKPOINT_STORAGE_MAX_RETAINED` (`ServerConfigOptions.java:232`) is documented as **"The maximum number of retained checkpoints"** (default `20`) — i.e. the user expectation is that storage never holds more than that many at once.

However, cleanup only fires when the in-memory `completedCheckpointIds` deque size is an *exact multiple* of `max-retained` **and** that multiple is `> 1`. It then deletes a whole batch of `max-retained` entries at once, rather than trimming down to the limit continuously.

For example, with `max-retained = 3`:

| checkpoints completed | deque size | checkpoints actually in storage |
|---|---|---|
| 1–3 | 1–3 | 1, 2, 3 (no cleanup — `3/3 = 1`, not `> 1`) |
| 4, 5 | 4, 5 | up to **5** retained — already over the configured limit of 3 |
| 6 | 6 → cleanup fires | deletes 1, 2, 3 → back down to 3 |
| 7, 8 | 7, 8 | up to **5** again |
| 9 | cleanup fires | back to 3 |

Instead of a hard cap of `max-retained`, storage oscillates between `max-retained` and `2 * max-retained - 1`. With the default of 20, up to **39** checkpoints can accumulate in storage before a single batch delete — nearly double the configured limit.

## SeaTunnel Version

dev (current HEAD: 3ac7003fb90d35e5945f94834742247fabd3dec7)

## SeaTunnel Config

```yaml
seatunnel:
  engine:
    checkpoint:
      storage:
        max-retained: 3
```

## Running Command / reproduction

Minimal reproduction of the branch logic in isolation:

```java
Deque<String> completedCheckpointIds = new ArrayDeque<>();
int maxRetained = 3;
int peakSize = 0;

for (int checkpointId = 1; checkpointId <= 30; checkpointId++) {
    completedCheckpointIds.addLast(String.valueOf(checkpointId));
    peakSize = Math.max(peakSize, completedCheckpointIds.size());
    if (completedCheckpointIds.size() % maxRetained == 0
            && completedCheckpointIds.size() / maxRetained > 1) {
        for (int i = 0; i < maxRetained; i++) {
            completedCheckpointIds.removeFirst();
        }
    }
}
System.out.println("configured max-retained = " + maxRetained);
System.out.println("peak retained observed = " + peakSize); // prints 5, not 3
```

Or integration-style: configure `checkpoint.storage.max-retained: 3`, run a streaming job long enough to complete checkpoints 4 and 5, and list the checkpoint storage directory between checkpoint 5 and 6 — it contains 5 checkpoints instead of the configured limit of 3.

## Suggested Fix

Drop the batch/modulo logic and trim down to `maxRetained` every time:

```java
int maxRetained = coordinatorConfig.getStorage().getMaxRetainedCheckpoints();
if (completedCheckpointIds.size() > maxRetained) {
    List<String> needDeleteCheckpointId = new ArrayList<>();
    while (completedCheckpointIds.size() > maxRetained) {
        needDeleteCheckpointId.add(completedCheckpointIds.removeFirst());
    }
    checkpointStorage.deleteCheckpoint(
            String.valueOf(completedCheckpoint.getJobId()),
            String.valueOf(completedCheckpoint.getPipelineId()),
            needDeleteCheckpointId);
}
```

This enforces the documented invariant ("at most `max-retained` checkpoints") after every single checkpoint completion.

## Java or Scala Version

JDK 17

