### Before reporting a bug

- [x] I have searched open and closed [GitHub issues](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/issues).
- [x] I have read the [ShardingSphere documentation](https://shardingsphere.apache.org/document/current/en/overview).

### ShardingSphere version

master, commit [`01446d4fad4`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/commit/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df)

### ShardingSphere project

ShardingSphere-Proxy

### Expected behavior

The proxy should execute statements in a transaction with [`isc_tpb_autocommit`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/tra.cpp#L3312-L3314) as the Firebird server does. The Firebird server [commits the transaction with retaining](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/jrd.cpp#L1400-L1411) only after a statement that changed data ([`VIO_store`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/vio.cpp#L4374-L4375), [`VIO_modify`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/vio.cpp#L3745-L3746), [`VIO_erase`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/vio.cpp#L2443-L2444)) or metadata ([`JRD_autocommit_ddl`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/jrd.cpp#L9361-L9382)). A SELECT does not end the transaction, so its cursor stays open and the client can fetch its rows.

### Actual behavior

In a transaction with [`isc_tpb_autocommit`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/tra.cpp#L3312-L3314) the proxy closes the cursor of a SELECT before the client fetches its rows.

The same statements were executed directly on the Firebird server and through the proxy, and the rows were read directly from the server:

| Steps | Firebird server | Proxy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Insert a row with ID = 8991 | Success, 1 row | Success, 1 row |
| Select the row with ID = 8991 | The row with ID = 8991 | Error [`isc_random`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/include/firebird/impl/msg/jrd.h#L63) "The result set is closed" |
| Insert a row with ID = 8992 | Success, 1 row | Success, 1 row |
| Read the rows with ID = 8991 and ID = 8992 directly on the server | Both rows are saved | Both rows are saved |

So through the proxy an application that uses Firebird auto-commit can change data, but cannot read any rows with SELECT.

### Reason analysis

- [`FirebirdStartTransactionPacket#isAutoCommit`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/database/protocol/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/database/protocol/firebird/packet/command/query/transaction/FirebirdStartTransactionPacket.java#L48-L50) reads [`isc_tpb_autocommit`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/tra.cpp#L3312-L3314), and [`FirebirdStartTransactionCommandExecutor#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/transaction/FirebirdStartTransactionCommandExecutor.java#L46) puts it into [`ConnectionSession`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/session/ConnectionSession.java#L57). So the proxy runs the statements of this transaction in auto-commit mode, outside a transaction.
- [`FirebirdExecuteStatementCommandExecutor#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/statement/execute/FirebirdExecuteStatementCommandExecutor.java#L88-L89) keeps the handler of the SELECT for the next [`op_fetch`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L5253-L5255) and marks it as in use. But after each command [`CommandExecutorTask#finish`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/command/CommandExecutorTask.java#L162) calls [`ProxyDatabaseConnectionManager#closeExecutionResources`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/connector/ProxyDatabaseConnectionManager.java#L326-L341), which outside a transaction [closes](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/connector/ProxyDatabaseConnectionManager.java#L329-L331) the handlers in use too.
- The client reads the rows with separate [`op_fetch`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L5253-L5255) packets. When the first one comes, [`FirebirdFetchStatementCommandExecutor#getQueryRowPacket`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/statement/fetch/FirebirdFetchStatementCommandExecutor.java#L71-L84) reads the closed result set of the backend, and the backend Jaybird [throws](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBResultSet.java#L336-L340) "The result set is closed".

### Steps to reproduce

#### Environment

- Firebird server 5.0.
- Client: Jaybird 6.0.7.

#### Steps

1. Create the table on the Firebird server:

```sql
CREATE TABLE T_PLAIN (ID INTEGER NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, NAME VARCHAR(20));
```

2. Start ShardingSphere-Proxy with this configuration:

```yaml
# global.yaml
authority:
  users:
    - user: root@%
      password: root
  privilege:
    type: ALL_PERMITTED
props:
  proxy-frontend-database-protocol-type: Firebird
```

```yaml
# database-firebird.yaml
databaseName: firebird_db
dataSources:
  ds_0:
    url: jdbc:firebirdsql://<host>/<database file>?encoding=UTF8
    username: <user>
    password: <password>
rules:
  - !SINGLE
    tables:
      - "*.*"
```

3. Connect with the Jaybird property [`useFirebirdAutocommit`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v6.0.7/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jaybird/props/PropertyNames.java#L74)`=true` and keep auto-commit on, so that Jaybird [adds](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v6.0.7/src/main/org/firebirdsql/gds/TransactionParameterBuffer.java#L99-L107) [`isc_tpb_autocommit`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/jrd/tra.cpp#L3312-L3314) to the transaction. Execute the steps from the table under "Actual behavior" through the proxy and directly on the Firebird server, and compare the results. "Example code" runs all of them.

### Example code

All cases on Jaybird 6.0.7, one method per case. The comment above each method shows what it prints against the Firebird server and through the proxy.

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.Statement;
import java.util.Properties;

public class FirebirdAutocommit {

    // The proxy; put the URL, user and password of the Firebird server here to compare
    static final String URL = "jdbc:firebirdsql://<proxy host>:<proxy port>/firebird_db?encoding=UTF8&wireCrypt=DISABLED";

    static final String USER = "root";

    static final String PASSWORD = "root";

    // The Firebird server, to read the rows directly
    static final String SERVER_URL = "jdbc:firebirdsql://<host>/<database file>";

    static final String SERVER_USER = "<user>";

    static final String SERVER_PASSWORD = "<password>";

    public static void main(final String[] args) throws Exception {
        executeOnServer("DELETE FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID BETWEEN 8991 AND 8992");
        firebirdAutocommit();
        executeOnServer("DELETE FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID BETWEEN 8991 AND 8992");
    }

    // Firebird server: "INSERT: 1 row", "SELECT: ID = 8991", "INSERT: 1 row",
    // "ID = 8991: the row is saved", "ID = 8992: the row is saved";
    // proxy: "INSERT: 1 row", "SELECT: The result set is closed [SQLState:HY000, ISC error code:335544382]", "INSERT: 1 row",
    // "ID = 8991: the row is saved", "ID = 8992: the row is saved"
    static void firebirdAutocommit() throws SQLException {
        Properties properties = new Properties();
        properties.setProperty("user", USER);
        properties.setProperty("password", PASSWORD);
        // In auto-commit mode Jaybird starts the transaction with isc_tpb_autocommit
        properties.setProperty("useFirebirdAutocommit", "true");
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(URL, properties)) {
            update(connection, "INSERT INTO T_PLAIN (ID, NAME) VALUES (8991, 'autocommit')");
            try (Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
                 ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery("SELECT ID, NAME FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = 8991")) {
                System.out.println("SELECT: " + (resultSet.next() ? "ID = " + resultSet.getInt(1) : "no row"));
            } catch (final SQLException ex) {
                System.out.println("SELECT: " + ex.getMessage());
            }
            update(connection, "INSERT INTO T_PLAIN (ID, NAME) VALUES (8992, 'autocommit 2')");
        }
        printRow(8991);
        printRow(8992);
    }

    static void update(final Connection connection, final String sql) throws SQLException {
        try (Statement statement = connection.createStatement()) {
            System.out.println("INSERT: " + statement.executeUpdate(sql) + " row");
        }
    }

    // Reads the row directly on the Firebird server
    static void printRow(final int id) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(SERVER_URL, SERVER_USER, SERVER_PASSWORD);
             ResultSet resultSet = connection.createStatement().executeQuery("SELECT NAME FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = " + id)) {
            System.out.println("ID = " + id + ": " + (resultSet.next() ? "the row is saved" : "the row is lost"));
        }
    }

    static void executeOnServer(final String sql) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(SERVER_URL, SERVER_USER, SERVER_PASSWORD)) {
            connection.createStatement().execute(sql);
        }
    }
}
