### Before reporting a bug

- [x] I have searched open and closed [GitHub issues](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/issues).
- [x] I have read the [ShardingSphere documentation](https://shardingsphere.apache.org/document/current/en/overview).

### ShardingSphere version

master, commit [`01446d4fad4`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/commit/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df)

### ShardingSphere project

ShardingSphere-Proxy

### Expected behavior

The proxy should execute [`COMMIT`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/parse.y#L5335-L5338) sent as an SQL statement as the Firebird server does. The server [commits the transaction](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/dsql/StmtNodes.cpp#L9669-L9678) of the statement and [releases its handle](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L4003-L4008), so the next statement with this handle is rejected.

### Actual behavior

The proxy commits its backend transaction, but keeps the client transaction handle valid and executes the next statements with it.

The same steps were executed directly on the Firebird server and through the proxy on a connection with auto-commit off, and the rows were read directly from the server:

| Steps | Firebird server | Proxy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Insert a row with ID = 9001 | Success | Success |
| Execute `COMMIT` as an SQL statement ([`Statement.execute`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBStatement.java#L578-L583)) | Success | Success |
| Insert a row with ID = 9002 | Error [`isc_bad_trans_handle`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/remote.h#L1501-L1504) "invalid transaction handle (expecting explicit transaction start)" | Success |
| Roll back ([`Connection.rollback()`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBConnection.java#L404-L420)) | Jaybird has already closed the connection after the previous error, because it [treats](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jaybird/xca/FatalErrorHelper.java#L84-L91) SQLSTATE [08003](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/resources/isc_error_sqlstates.properties#L12) of `isc_bad_trans_handle` as fatal, and [reports](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v5.0.13/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBConnection.java#L407-L410) "You cannot rollback closed connection." | Success |
| Read the rows with ID = 9001 and ID = 9002 | The row with ID = 9001 is saved, the row with ID = 9002 is not saved | The row with ID = 9001 is saved, the row with ID = 9002 is not saved |

`ROLLBACK` executed as an SQL statement leaves the handle valid in the same way. The Firebird server rejects the next INSERT with [`isc_bad_trans_handle`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/remote.h#L1501-L1504), while the proxy [rolls back](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/handler/tcl/local/type/RollbackProxyBackendHandler.java#L44-L46) only the backend transaction and saves the row of this INSERT.

So through the proxy an application keeps writing in a transaction that it has already committed with `COMMIT`, while the Firebird server rejects these statements. No error tells the application that its transaction has ended.

### Reason analysis

- The proxy executes the `COMMIT` statement of [`op_execute`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L5243-L5245) with [`CommitProxyBackendHandler#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/backend/core/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/backend/handler/tcl/local/type/CommitProxyBackendHandler.java#L52-L56), which commits only the backend transaction.
- The client transaction handle is removed from [`FirebirdTransactionIdGenerator`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/transaction/FirebirdTransactionIdGenerator.java#L32) by [`FirebirdTransactionIdGenerator#closeTransaction`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/transaction/FirebirdTransactionIdGenerator.java#L98-L100). Only [`FirebirdCommitTransactionCommandExecutor#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/transaction/FirebirdCommitTransactionCommandExecutor.java#L52) for [`op_commit`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L3413-L3415) and [`FirebirdRollbackTransactionCommandExecutor#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/transaction/FirebirdRollbackTransactionCommandExecutor.java#L51) for [`op_rollback`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L3417-L3419) call it, so after the `COMMIT` statement the handle stays active.
- The Firebird server rejects the released handle already in [`op_prepare_statement`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/firebird/blob/v5.0.4/src/remote/server/server.cpp#L5262-L5264), but [`FirebirdPrepareStatementCommandExecutor#execute`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/statement/prepare/FirebirdPrepareStatementCommandExecutor.java#L113-L127) does not check the transaction handle. [`FirebirdExecuteStatementCommandExecutor#validateTransactionHandle`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/statement/execute/FirebirdExecuteStatementCommandExecutor.java#L105-L108) and [`FirebirdRollbackTransactionCommandExecutor#validateTransactionHandle`](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere/blob/01446d4fad48450e7fc3eb3b3631b25d6bf411df/proxy/frontend/dialect/firebird/src/main/java/org/apache/shardingsphere/proxy/frontend/firebird/command/query/transaction/FirebirdRollbackTransactionCommandExecutor.java#L55-L59) accept the old handle, so the next INSERT and the rollback succeed.

### Steps to reproduce

#### Environment

- Firebird server 5.0.
- Client: Jaybird 5.0.13.

#### Steps

1. Create the table on the Firebird server:

```sql
CREATE TABLE T_PLAIN (ID INTEGER NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY, NAME VARCHAR(20));
```

2. Start ShardingSphere-Proxy with this configuration:

```yaml
# global.yaml
authority:
  users:
    - user: root@%
      password: root
  privilege:
    type: ALL_PERMITTED
props:
  proxy-frontend-database-protocol-type: Firebird
```

```yaml
# database-firebird.yaml
databaseName: firebird_db
dataSources:
  ds_0:
    url: jdbc:firebirdsql://<host>/<database file>?encoding=UTF8
    username: <user>
    password: <password>
rules:
  - !SINGLE
    tables:
      - "*.*"
```

3. Use Jaybird 5.0.13, which sends `COMMIT` to the server as an SQL statement. Jaybird 6 does not do this. By default it rejects the statement ([`allowTxStmts`](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v6.0.7/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jaybird/props/PropertyConstants.java#L60) is `false`), and with `allowTxStmts=true` it [commits](https://github.com/FirebirdSQL/jaybird/blob/v6.0.7/src/main/org/firebirdsql/jdbc/FBConnection.java#L441-L447) with `op_commit` instead. Execute the steps from the table under "Actual behavior" through the proxy and directly on the Firebird server, and compare the results. "Example code" runs all of them.

### Example code

All cases on Jaybird 5.0.13, one method per case. The comment above each method shows what it prints against the Firebird server and through the proxy.

```java
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.DriverManager;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.Statement;

public class CommitStatement {

    // The proxy; put the URL, user and password of the Firebird server here to compare
    static final String URL = "jdbc:firebirdsql://<proxy host>:<proxy port>/firebird_db?encoding=UTF8&wireCrypt=DISABLED";

    static final String USER = "root";

    static final String PASSWORD = "root";

    // The Firebird server, to read the rows directly
    static final String SERVER_URL = "jdbc:firebirdsql://<host>/<database file>";

    static final String SERVER_USER = "<user>";

    static final String SERVER_PASSWORD = "<password>";

    public static void main(final String[] args) throws Exception {
        executeOnServer("DELETE FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID BETWEEN 9001 AND 9002");
        commitStatement();
        executeOnServer("DELETE FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID BETWEEN 9001 AND 9002");
    }

    // Firebird server: "INSERT 9001: success", "COMMIT: success",
    // "INSERT 9002: invalid transaction handle (expecting explicit transaction start) [SQLState:08003, ISC error code:335544332]",
    // "rollback: You cannot rollback closed connection.", "ID = 9001: the row is saved", "ID = 9002: the row is not saved";
    // proxy: "INSERT 9001: success", "COMMIT: success", "INSERT 9002: success", "rollback: success",
    // "ID = 9001: the row is saved", "ID = 9002: the row is not saved"
    static void commitStatement() throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(URL, USER, PASSWORD)) {
            connection.setAutoCommit(false);
            execute(connection, "INSERT 9001", "INSERT INTO T_PLAIN (ID, NAME) VALUES (9001, 'before')");
            // Jaybird 5 sends COMMIT as an SQL statement in the transaction of the connection
            execute(connection, "COMMIT", "COMMIT");
            // The same transaction handle as before COMMIT
            execute(connection, "INSERT 9002", "INSERT INTO T_PLAIN (ID, NAME) VALUES (9002, 'after')");
            try {
                connection.rollback();
                System.out.println("rollback: success");
            } catch (final SQLException ex) {
                System.out.println("rollback: " + ex.getMessage());
            }
        }
        printRow(9001);
        printRow(9002);
    }

    static void execute(final Connection connection, final String step, final String sql) {
        try (Statement statement = connection.createStatement()) {
            statement.execute(sql);
            System.out.println(step + ": success");
        } catch (final SQLException ex) {
            System.out.println(step + ": " + ex.getMessage());
        }
    }

    // Reads the row directly on the Firebird server
    static void printRow(final int id) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(SERVER_URL, SERVER_USER, SERVER_PASSWORD);
             ResultSet resultSet = connection.createStatement().executeQuery("SELECT NAME FROM T_PLAIN WHERE ID = " + id)) {
            System.out.println("ID = " + id + ": " + (resultSet.next() ? "the row is saved" : "the row is not saved"));
        }
    }

    static void executeOnServer(final String sql) throws SQLException {
        try (Connection connection = DriverManager.getConnection(SERVER_URL, SERVER_USER, SERVER_PASSWORD)) {
            connection.createStatement().execute(sql);
        }
    }
}
